<interrogationes unitas="arbor_lexema">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a C source buffer into a list of tokens?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexator_creare arbor_lexema_omnia arbor_lexema_proximum internamentum_globale">Create
      a lexer with arbor_lexator_creare(piscina, intern, fons,
      mensura) (fons need not be NUL-terminated; intern can be
      internamentum_globale()), then call arbor_lexema_omnia for a Xar
      of ArborLexema POINTERS: read each with *(ArborLexema**)
      xar_obtinere(x, i). Or call arbor_lexema_proximum in a loop until
      genus is ARBOR_LEXEMA_EOF. Every token is allocated in the
      piscina.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I free the source buffer while I still use the tokens?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexator_creare chorda_internare">Yes for
      the text: every token valor and trivia valor is interned
      through chorda_internare, which copies it, so it lives as long
      as the intern table. byte_offset and longitudo still refer to
      the original buffer, so anything that slices the source by
      offset needs it kept. Zero-length tokens (EOF) have valor.datum
      NIHIL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get at the comments and whitespace around a token?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexema_proximum ArborTrivia" via="lib/arbor_syntaxis.c">Each
      ArborLexema has trivia_ante (everything since the previous
      token's trailing trivia, newlines included) and trivia_post
      (spaces, tabs and comments that start on the token's line,
      stopping before the next newline; a block comment there is
      taken whole even if it spans lines). Either is NIHIL when empty. Despite the header
      comment saying Xar of ArborTrivia, both hold ArborTrivia
      POINTERS: read *(ArborTrivia**)xar_obtinere(t, i), as
      arbor_syntaxis does. est_commentum and est_c99 (a // comment)
      tell the kinds apart.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I look ahead with arbor_lexema_prospicere?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexema_prospicere arbor_lexema_omnia">No.
      It is an unimplemented stub (a TODO in lib/arbor_lexema.c) that
      always returns NIHIL, whatever the header says. Tokenize
      everything with arbor_lexema_omnia and index the Xar
      instead.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does arbor_lexema_omnia stop before the end of my file?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexema_omnia arbor_lexema_proximum">It
      stops at the first ARBOR_LEXEMA_ERROR token as well as at EOF,
      and then there is NO EOF token in the Xar. ERROR is any byte
      the lexer does not know outside comments and literals: @, $, `,
      a backslash (so a backslash-newline line continuation in a
      #define), any control byte other than tab, newline and carriage
      return (form feed, vertical tab, NUL...), or any byte of 0x80
      and above. The ERROR token itself is the last element, so check
      its genus before trusting the list. arbor_lexema_proximum does
      not stop there: call it in your own loop up to EOF if you need
      the rest of the file.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can the lexer read every C89 numeric literal?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexema_proximum">Most, not all.
      Decimal, octal and hex integers with U/L/LL suffixes and floats
      like 3.14, 1e10 and 2.5e+3f work. A float with nothing after the
      point or nothing before it does not: 1. lexes as INTEGER then
      PUNCTUM, .5 as PUNCTUM then INTEGER, and 1.f as INTEGER,
      PUNCTUM, IDENTIFICATOR. An octal-looking prefix stops the
      fraction too (01.5 is INTEGER 01, PUNCTUM, INTEGER 5), and 08
      splits into two INTEGER tokens with no error. Hex floats are C99
      and not handled.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does an unterminated string or comment produce?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_lexema_proximum">No error. An unclosed
      string or character literal ends just before the newline and is
      still a STRING_LIT or CHAR_LIT; an unclosed block comment runs
      to the end of input as ordinary trivia. Nothing in the token
      marks it, so a consumer that cares must check the final quote
      or the closing star-slash itself.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>which code uses arbor_lexema, and is it the lexer silva uses?
    <responsum symbolum="arbor_praeparator_creare arbor2_lexator_creare" via="lib/arbor.c lib/arbor_praeparator.c lib/arbor_syntaxis.c">It
      feeds the first arbor pipeline: arbor.c, the preprocessor in
      arbor_praeparator (which also lexes macro values and included
      files with it) and arbor_syntaxis. No silva source file
      includes it. arbor2_lexema is a separate fork for arbor-v2 that
      adds newline tokens and line-continuation tracking. Here linea
      and columna are 1-based and count bytes, and the standard field
      is always ARBOR_STANDARD_C89 (nothing sets another
      value).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
